{"severity":"hint","file":"errors/cap_root_broad_1.march","start_line":18,"start_col":2,"end_line":18,"end_col":27,"message":"This declaration uses `Cap(IO)` (the root capability); consider narrowing to e.g. `Cap(IO.FileRead)` or `Cap(IO.Console)` for least-privilege.","code":"cap_root_broad","fix":null,"labels":[],"notes":[],"category":"CAPABILITY"}
{"severity":"error","file":"errors/cap_root_broad_1.march","start_line":19,"start_col":9,"end_line":19,"end_col":13,"message":"`Cap(IO)` cannot be deserialized — a capability may only be received, never constructed, so `Loot` cannot derive Json.\nhint: hold the capability in a separate value and pass it as a parameter, keeping `Loot` free of capability fields.","code":"cap_deserialize","fix":null,"labels":[],"notes":[],"category":"CAPABILITY"}
{"severity":"error","file":"errors/cap_root_broad_1.march","start_line":21,"start_col":2,"end_line":23,"end_col":5,"message":"`main` performs IO but declares no grant. The program reaches `IO.Console`; a `main` with no capability parameter is granted nothing.\nhelp: declare the grant `main` actually needs —\n        fn main(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console)) : ()\nor grant everything with `fn main(cap : Cap(IO))`.\n`forge fix` can apply this.","code":"cap_grant","fix":{"kind":"replace","start_line":21,"start_col":9,"end_line":21,"end_col":11,"text":"(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console))"},"labels":[],"notes":[],"category":"CAPABILITY"}
